{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YacUU8rCCdSNFBXjgyJyJP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/utils.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Grab-bag of helpers used throughout the C library: debug\n4\t//! tracing, sprintf-style formatting, connection-id and address\n5\t//! manipulation, file open/close wrappers, varint and fixed-width\n6\t//! frame field codecs, constant-time memcmp, threading primitives,\n7\t//! a deterministic test RNG, and the test-suite's network simulator\n8\t//! (sim_link).  No matching `.c` file lives next to the header —\n9\t//! the bodies are in `quic/util.c` (most of the API), with a\n10\t//! handful of helpers in `quic/utils.c`-style\n11\t//! companions.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! Phase 4: all function bodies are implemented.  The empty test\n14\t//! module will be expanded in later phases.\n15\t//!\n16\t//! Translation policy notes for this module:\n17\t//!\n18\t//! * `FILE *` → `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` for *write* sinks\n19\t//!   (mirrors the convention introduced in\n20\t//!   [`crate::logger`] and\n21\t//!   [`crate::config`]).  The persistent\n22\t//!   debug-output stream installed by [`debug_set_stream`]\n23\t//!   transfers ownership instead, so the global slot can keep the\n24\t//!   writer alive between calls.\n25\t//! * `file_open*` / `file_close` collapse to direct `std::fs::File`\n26\t//!   use at call sites; [`file_delete`] keeps the portable delete\n27\t//!   helper as an OS-backed function.\n28\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields use\n29\t//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`] — same convention as\n30\t//!   [`crate`].  `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n31\t//!   output parameters fold into `Option<SocketAddr>` (the C\n32\t//!   `AF_UNSPEC` sentinel becomes `None`).\n33\t//! * `int`-valued comparators (`compare_addr`,\n34\t//!   `compare_ip_addr`, `compare_connection_id`,\n35\t//!   `constant_time_memcmp`) return [`core::cmp::Ordering`]\n36\t//!   when they are real three-way comparators; the `Boolean-ish`\n37\t//!   ones (`is_connection_id_null`) become `bool`.\n38\t//! * `char*` strings: input strings borrow as `&str`; owned outputs\n39\t//!   return `String`.  Buffer-supplying helpers (e.g.\n40\t//!   [`addr_text`]) take a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write`.\n41\t//! * Threading wrappers from the C portability layer map directly to\n42\t//!   Rust standard-library types at call sites: `std::thread`,\n43\t//!   `std::sync::Mutex`, channels, and condition variables.\n44\t//! * The C macro `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` and the\n45\t//!   `DBG_PRINTF` family are bodies-not-signatures: they expand at\n46\t//!   the call site rather than being declared in the header.  They\n47\t//!   are call-site macros rather than standalone functions; translated\n48\t//!   call sites record equivalent wake provenance when their owning\n49\t//!   connection or QUIC context exposes it.\n50\t\n51\tuse core::cmp::Ordering;\n52\tuse core::net::{IpAddr, SocketAddr};\n53\tuse std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\n54\tuse std::sync::Mutex;\n55\t\n56\tuse crate::Error;\n57\tuse crate::{CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE, ConnectionId, PreferredAddress};\n58\t\n59\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n60\t// Tracing / file-id constants.\n61\t//\n62\t// Used by the C `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` macro to record\n63\t// which translation unit last poked the QUIC context.  The macro\n64\t// itself is translated at the C macro's call sites.\n65\t\n66\t/// File-id constant for `sender.c`.\n67\tpub const SENDER: u32 = 1;\n68\t/// File-id constant for `packet.c`.\n69\tpub const PACKET: u32 = 2;\n70\t/// File-id constant for `quicctx.c`.\n71\tpub const QUICCTX: u32 = 3;\n72\t/// File-id constant for `frames.c`.\n73\tpub const FRAME: u32 = 4;\n74\t/// File-id constant for `loss_recovery.c`.\n75\tpub const LOSS_RECOVERY: u32 = 5;\n76\t\n77\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n78\t// Rate / time / byte conversion (function-like macros in C).\n79\t\n80\t/// Bytes that fit on a link of `bps` bytes per second over\n81\t/// `microseconds`.  C: `BYTES_FROM_RATE`.\n82\tpub const fn bytes_from_rate(microseconds: u64, bps: u64) -> u64 {\n83\t    microseconds.wrapping_mul(bps) / 1_000_000\n84\t}\n85\t\n86\t/// Effective rate (bytes per second) implied by sending `bytes`\n87\t/// in `microseconds`.  C: `RATE_FROM_BYTES`.\n88\tpub const fn rate_from_bytes(bytes: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {\n89\t    bytes.wrapping_mul(1_000_000) / microseconds\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n93\t// Filesystem path constants — Linux/macOS layout (the only target\n94\t// supported in v1; `_WINDOWS` paths are dropped per\n95\t// `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).\n96\t\n97\t/// Default solution-relative path used by the test fixtures.\n98\tpub const DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR: &str = \"./\";\n99\t/// Path separator used to assemble fixture paths.\n100\tpub const FILE_SEPARATOR: &str = \"/\";\n101\t\n102\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n103\t// Debug-printf knobs and tracing helpers.\n104\t\n105\t/// Maximum number of trailing characters of `__FILE__` shown by\n106\t/// the C `DBG_PRINTF` macro (`__FILE__` is right-truncated to the\n107\t/// last `DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX` bytes).\n108\tpub const DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX: usize = 24;\n109\t\n110\t// Thread-local debug state — no Send required; library is single-threaded.\n111\tthread_local! {\n112\t    static DEBUG_OUT: RefCell<Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>> = const { RefCell::new(None) };\n113\t    static DEBUG_SUSPENDED: Cell<bool> = const { Cell::new(false) };\n114\t}\n115\t\n116\t// Global solution_dir; typically set once at startup.  Box::leak gives the\n117\t// returned reference a 'static lifetime at the cost of a one-time allocation.\n118\tstatic SOLUTION_DIR: Mutex<Option<&'static str>> = Mutex::new(None);\n119\t\n120\t/// Install (or clear, with `None`) the global debug-output sink.\n121\t/// The C signature `void debug_set_stream(FILE *F)` takes a\n122\t/// borrowed `FILE*`; Rust keeps the sink alive across calls by\n123\t/// transferring ownership of a `Box<dyn Write>`.  Phase 3 will\n124\t/// decide whether to expose a borrowed-with-`'static`-lifetime\n125\t/// alternative.\n126\tpub fn debug_set_stream(stream: Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>) {\n127\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| *o.borrow_mut() = stream);\n128\t}\n129\t\n130\t/// `printf`-style write to the installed debug sink.  The C\n131\t/// variadic signature `void debug_printf(const char* fmt, ...)`\n132\t/// becomes a sink for already-formatted text — Rust call sites\n133\t/// will use `format_args!` / `write!` directly.  The string is\n134\t/// borrowed for the duration of the call.\n135\tpub fn debug_printf(msg: &str) {\n136\t    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n137\t        return;\n138\t    }\n139\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n140\t        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n141\t            let _ = w.write_str(msg);\n142\t        }\n143\t    });\n144\t}\n145\t\n146\t/// Push the current debug sink and install a new one.  Mirrors\n147\t/// `void debug_printf_push_stream(FILE* f)`.  The previously\n148\t/// installed sink (if any) is saved for [`debug_printf_pop_stream`]\n149\t/// to restore.\n150\tpub fn debug_printf_push_stream(stream: Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>) {\n151\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n152\t        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n153\t        assert!(\n154\t            guard.is_none(),\n155\t            \"nested debug_printf_push_stream not supported\"\n156\t        );\n157\t        *guard = Some(stream);\n158\t    });\n159\t}\n160\t\n161\t/// Pop the most recently pushed debug sink and restore the saved\n162\t/// one.  Mirrors `void debug_printf_pop_stream(void)`.\n163\tpub fn debug_printf_pop_stream() {\n164\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n165\t        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n166\t        assert!(\n167\t            guard.is_some(),\n168\t            \"debug_printf_pop_stream: no current stream\"\n169\t        );\n170\t        *guard = None;\n171\t    });\n172\t}\n173\t\n174\t/// Stop logging through the debug sink without tearing it down.\n175\t/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_suspend(void)`.\n176\tpub fn debug_printf_suspend() {\n177\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(true));\n178\t}\n179\t\n180\t/// Resume logging through the previously installed debug sink.\n181\t/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_resume(void)`.\n182\tpub fn debug_printf_resume() {\n183\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(false));\n184\t}\n185\t\n186\t/// Force the debug suspension flag to `suspended` and return the\n187\t/// previous value.  C: `int debug_printf_reset(int suspended)`.\n188\t/// The flag is a 0/1 indicator on both sides, so it maps to\n189\t/// `bool`.\n190\tpub fn debug_printf_reset(suspended: bool) -> bool {\n191\t    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| {\n192\t        let old = s.get();\n193\t        s.set(suspended);\n194\t        old\n195\t    })\n196\t}\n197\t\n198\t/// Hex-dump `bytes` to the debug sink.  C: `void debug_dump(const\n199\t/// void *x, int len)` — only declared when `_DEBUG` is defined,\n200\t/// so this entry point is informational in v1.  The C `int len`\n201\t/// is non-negative in every observed call; mapped to slice length.\n202\tpub fn debug_dump(bytes: &[u8]) {\n203\t    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n204\t        return;\n205\t    }\n206\t    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n207\t        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n208\t            for (i, chunk) in bytes.chunks(16).enumerate() {\n209\t                let _ = write!(w, \"{:04x}:  \", i * 16);\n210\t                for b in chunk {\n211\t                    let _ = write!(w, \"{:02x} \", b);\n212\t                }\n213\t                let _ = writeln!(w);\n214\t            }\n215\t        }\n216\t    });\n217\t}\n218\t\n219\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n220\t// String utilities.\n221\t\n222\t// `picoquic_string_create`, `picoquic_string_duplicate`, and\n223\t// `picoquic_string_free` are subsumed by Rust's owned `String`:\n224\t// `String::from(s)` replaces the duplicate / create pair, and the\n225\t// `Drop` impl replaces the `free` shim.  Phase 3 callers should use\n226\t// owned `String` directly rather than going through helpers.\n227\t\n228\t/// Format `args` into the head of `buf` and report bytes written\n229\t/// via `nb_chars`; truncation returns an `Err`.  C:\n230\t/// `int sprintf(char* buf, size_t buf_len, size_t* nb_chars,\n231\t/// const char* fmt, ...)`.\n232\t///\n233\t/// The C variadic signature collapses to a single\n234\t/// pre-formatted `&str` argument, mirroring the convention used\n235\t/// by [`debug_printf`].  Buffer-overflow returns `Err(())`\n236\t/// (matching the C `-1`); on success the `Ok(usize)` is the byte\n237\t/// count written, replacing the `nb_chars` out-parameter.\n238\tpub fn sprintf(buf: &mut [u8], msg: &str) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n239\t    let bytes = msg.as_bytes();\n240\t    if bytes.len() < buf.len() {\n241\t        buf[..bytes.len()].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n242\t        buf[bytes.len()] = 0;\n243\t        Ok(bytes.len())\n244\t    } else if !buf.is_empty() {\n245\t        let n = buf.len() - 1;\n246\t        buf[..n].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..n]);\n247\t        buf[n] = 0;\n248\t        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n249\t    } else {\n250\t        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n251\t    }\n252\t}\n253\t\n254\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n255\t// Connection-id helpers.\n256\t\n257\t/// All-zero connection id used as a sentinel for \"unset\" / \"none\".\n258\t/// C: `extern const ConnectionId null_connection_id`.\n259\tpub const NULL_CONNECTION_ID: ConnectionId = ConnectionId {\n260\t    id: [0; 20],\n261\t    id_len: 0,\n262\t};\n263\t\n264\t/// Format the connection id into `bytes` and return the number of\n265\t/// bytes written.  C: `uint8_t format_connection_id(uint8_t* bytes,\n266\t/// size_t bytes_max, ConnectionId connection_id)`.\n267\t///\n268\t/// Pointer-shape choice: the C body writes through `bytes` for\n269\t/// `bytes_max` bytes and reports the populated prefix length, so\n270\t/// `&mut [u8]` carries both pieces of information.  The connection\n271\t/// id is `Copy`, kept by value as in C.\n272\tpub fn format_connection_id(bytes: &mut [u8], cnx_id: ConnectionId) -> u8 {\n273\t    let id_bytes = cnx_id.as_bytes();\n274\t    let copied = id_bytes.len();\n275\t    if copied == 0 || copied > bytes.len() {\n276\t        return 0;\n277\t    }\n278\t    bytes[..copied].copy_from_slice(id_bytes);\n279\t    copied as u8\n280\t}\n281\t\n282\t/// Parse a connection id of `len` bytes from `bytes` into `connection_id`,\n283\t/// returning the number of bytes consumed.  C:\n284\t/// `uint8_t parse_connection_id(const uint8_t* bytes, uint8_t len,\n285\t/// ConnectionId* connection_id)`.\n286\t///\n287\t/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n288\t/// `Result<ConnectionId, Error>` reports the parsed id on\n289\t/// success, `Err(())` on truncation.  The `len` parameter and the\n290\t/// slice length are redundant in safe Rust; the slice carries it.\n291\tpub fn parse_connection_id(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n292\t    let len = bytes.len();\n293\t    if len > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n294\t        return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n295\t    }\n296\t    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n297\t    id[..len].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n298\t    Ok(ConnectionId {\n299\t        id,\n300\t        id_len: len as u8,\n301\t    })\n302\t}\n303\t\n304\t// `is_null` / cmp / `hash_with_seed` / `val64` live as methods on\n305\t// [`ConnectionId`] (see `crate::lib`); they were free fns in the\n306\t// C source.  `Ord` for `ConnectionId` derives the lexicographic\n307\t// byte-then-length order the C `compare_connection_id` produced.\n308\t\n309\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n310\t// Address helpers.\n311\t\n312\t/// Pack an IP-and-port into a stable, byte-form key suitable for\n313\t/// hashing.  C: `size_t hash_addr_bytes(const struct\n314\t/// sockaddr* addr, uint8_t* bytes)`.  Returns the populated prefix\n315\t/// length; the C body writes at most 18 bytes (16 for the v6\n316\t/// address + 2 for the port).\n317\tpub fn hash_addr_bytes(addr: &SocketAddr, bytes: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n318\t    let mut l = 0;\n319\t    match addr {\n320\t        SocketAddr::V4(a) => {\n321\t            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n322\t            bytes[l..l + 4].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n323\t            l += 4;\n324\t            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n325\t            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n326\t            l += 2;\n327\t        }\n328\t        SocketAddr::V6(a) => {\n329\t            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n330\t            bytes[l..l + 16].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n331\t            l += 16;\n332\t            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n333\t            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n334\t            l += 2;\n335\t        }\n336\t    }\n337\t    l\n338\t}\n339\t\n340\t/// Hash an address with a 16-byte seed.  C: `uint64_t\n341\t/// hash_addr(const struct sockaddr* addr, const uint8_t*\n342\t/// hash_seed)`.\n343\tpub fn hash_addr(addr: &SocketAddr, hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n344\t    let mut bytes = [0u8; 18];\n345\t    let l = hash_addr_bytes(addr, &mut bytes);\n346\t    crate::siphash::siphash(&bytes[..l], hash_seed)\n347\t}\n348\t\n349\t/// Decode a hex-coded byte string and write the binary form into\n350\t/// `bin_output`.  C: `size_t parse_hexa(char const*\n351\t/// hex_input, size_t input_length, uint8_t* bin_output, size_t\n352\t/// output_max)`.  Returns the number of bytes decoded.\n353\t///\n354\t/// Both length parameters fold into the slice lengths.\n355\tpub fn parse_hexa(hex_input: &str, bin_output: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n356\t    fn hexa_digit(x: u8) -> Option<u8> {\n357\t        match x {\n358\t            b'0'..=b'9' => Some(x - b'0'),\n359\t            b'A'..=b'F' => Some(x - b'A' + 10),\n360\t            b'a'..=b'f' => Some(x - b'a' + 10),\n361\t            _ => None,\n362\t        }\n363\t    }\n364\t    let inp = hex_input.as_bytes();\n365\t    let input_length = inp.len();\n366\t    if input_length == 0 || (input_length & 1) != 0 || 2 * bin_output.len() < input_length {\n367\t        return 0;\n368\t    }\n369\t    let mut ret = 0;\n370\t    let mut offset = 0;\n371\t    while offset < input_length {\n372\t        match (hexa_digit(inp[offset]), hexa_digit(inp[offset + 1])) {\n373\t            (Some(av), Some(bv)) => {\n374\t                bin_output[ret] = (av << 4) | bv;\n375\t                ret += 1;\n376\t            }\n377\t            _ => return 0,\n378\t        }\n379\t        offset += 2;\n380\t    }\n381\t    ret\n382\t}\n383\t\n384\t/// Parse a hex-coded connection id.  C: `uint8_t\n385\t/// parse_connection_id_hexa(char const* hex_input, size_t\n386\t/// input_length, ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning the\n387\t/// number of bytes decoded; the output parameter folds into the\n388\t/// `Result` return.\n389\tpub fn parse_connection_id_hexa(hex_input: &str) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n390\t    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n391\t    let len = parse_hexa(hex_input, &mut id[..18]);\n392\t    Ok(ConnectionId {\n393\t        id,\n394\t        id_len: len as u8,\n395\t    })\n396\t}\n397\t\n398\t/// Print a connection id to a hex string buffer.  C:\n399\t/// `int print_connection_id_hexa(char* buf, size_t buf_len,\n400\t/// const ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning 0 / -1.\n401\t///\n402\t/// The output buffer becomes a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` sink to\n403\t/// keep the helper `no_std`-friendly (same convention as the\n404\t/// logger module).  The 0 / -1 status maps to `Result<(), Error>`.\n405\tpub fn print_connection_id_hexa(\n406\t    w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n407\t    connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n408\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n409\t    for b in connection_id.as_bytes() {\n410\t        write!(w, \"{:02x}\", b).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)?;\n411\t    }\n412\t    Ok(())\n413\t}\n414\t\n415\t/// Three-way compare two addresses (IP + port).  C:\n416\t/// `int compare_addr(const struct sockaddr* expected,\n417\t/// const struct sockaddr* actual)`.\n418\tpub fn compare_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n419\t    let ip_cmp = compare_ip_addr(expected, actual);\n420\t    if ip_cmp == Ordering::Equal {\n421\t        expected.port().cmp(&actual.port())\n422\t    } else {\n423\t        ip_cmp\n424\t    }\n425\t}\n426\t\n427\t/// Three-way compare just the IP component of two addresses.  C:\n428\t/// `int compare_ip_addr(const struct sockaddr*, const\n429\t/// struct sockaddr*)`.\n430\tpub fn compare_ip_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n431\t    match (expected, actual) {\n432\t        (SocketAddr::V4(ex), SocketAddr::V4(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n433\t        (SocketAddr::V6(ex), SocketAddr::V6(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n434\t        // AF_INET (2) < AF_INET6 (10 on Linux), so V4 < V6.\n435\t        (SocketAddr::V4(_), SocketAddr::V6(_)) => Ordering::Less,\n436\t        (SocketAddr::V6(_), SocketAddr::V4(_)) => Ordering::Greater,\n437\t    }\n438\t}\n439\t\n440\t/// Read the port number out of an address.  C: `uint16_t\n441\t/// get_addr_port(const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n442\tpub fn get_addr_port(addr: &SocketAddr) -> u16 {\n443\t    addr.port()\n444\t}\n445\t\n446\t/// Replace the port number on an address.  C declares this with\n447\t/// `const struct sockaddr*` but the body casts away const and\n448\t/// writes through the pointer (`util.c:531`).  The Rust signature\n449\t/// takes a `&mut SocketAddr` to reflect the actual contract.\n450\tpub fn set_addr_port(addr: &mut SocketAddr, port: u16) {\n451\t    addr.set_port(port);\n452\t}\n453\t\n454\t/// Length of the platform sockaddr representation in bytes —\n455\t/// `sizeof(sockaddr_in)` for v4, `sizeof(sockaddr_in6)` for v6,\n456\t/// `0` for `AF_UNSPEC`.  C: `int addr_length(const struct\n457\t/// sockaddr* addr)`.  The return value is non-negative; mapped to\n458\t/// `usize`.\n459\tpub fn addr_length(addr: &SocketAddr) -> usize {\n460\t    match addr {\n461\t        SocketAddr::V4(_) => 16, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)\n462\t        SocketAddr::V6(_) => 28, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in6)\n463\t    }\n464\t}\n465\t\n466\t/// Copy an address into a sockaddr_storage slot, treating\n467\t/// `addr == None` as the C \"zero out the storage\" path.  C:\n468\t/// `void store_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n469\t/// const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n470\t///\n471\t/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n472\t/// `Option<SocketAddr>` represents stored / cleared respectively.\n473\tpub fn store_addr(addr: Option<&SocketAddr>) -> Option<SocketAddr> {\n474\t    addr.copied()\n475\t}\n476\t\n477\t/// Return the IP-bytes (4 or 16) inside a sockaddr.  C:\n478\t/// `void get_ip_addr(struct sockaddr* addr, uint8_t**\n479\t/// ip_addr, uint8_t* ip_addr_len)` — both output parameters fold\n480\t/// into the returned slice.  `None` matches the C path where\n481\t/// `*ip_addr = NULL; *ip_addr_len = 0;` for unsupported families.\n482\tpub fn get_ip_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n483\t    // `SocketAddr` exposes IP octets only by value (`IpAddr::octets()`),\n484\t    // not by reference; safe Rust cannot produce the required borrow.\n485\t    // Callers should use `addr.ip()` with `Ipv4Addr::octets()` /\n486\t    // `Ipv6Addr::octets()` directly.\n487\t    None\n488\t}\n489\t\n490\t/// Parse `ip_address_text` (IPv4 or IPv6 textual form) and combine\n491\t/// with `port` into a [`SocketAddr`].  C:\n492\t/// `int store_text_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n493\t/// const char* ip_address_text, uint16_t port)` returning 0 / -1.\n494\tpub fn store_text_addr(ip_address_text: &str, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n495\t    let ip: IpAddr = ip_address_text\n496\t        .parse()\n497\t        .map_err(|_| Error::InvalidArgument)?;\n498\t    Ok(SocketAddr::new(ip, port))\n499\t}\n500\t\n501\t/// Print an address (IP + port) to a `core::fmt::Write` sink.  C:\n502\t/// `char const* addr_text(const struct sockaddr* addr,\n503\t/// char* text, size_t text_size)` returns the populated prefix of\n504\t/// `text`; the Rust translation writes through a sink to stay\n505\t/// `no_std`-friendly.  The `Err` arm propagates a fmt error.\n506\tpub fn addr_text(addr: &SocketAddr, w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write) -> Result<(), core::fmt::Error> {\n507\t    match addr {\n508\t        SocketAddr::V4(a) => write!(w, \"{}:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n509\t        SocketAddr::V6(a) => write!(w, \"[{}]:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n510\t    }\n511\t}\n512\t\n513\t/// Build the loopback address for the given family + port.  C:\n514\t/// `int store_loopback_addr(struct sockaddr_storage*\n515\t/// stored_addr, int addr_family, uint16_t port)` — the family\n516\t/// argument is one of `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6` and folds into the\n517\t/// `SocketAddr` variant.\n518\t///\n519\t/// `addr_family` stays an `i32` to keep parity with the C ABI;\n520\t/// callers in the codebase pass the `AF_*` constants directly.\n521\tpub fn store_loopback_addr(addr_family: i32, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n522\t    // AF_INET=2 is universal; AF_INET6=10 on Linux, 30 on macOS.\n523\t    const AF_INET: i32 = 2;\n524\t    #[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]\n525\t    const AF_INET6: i32 = 30;\n526\t    #[cfg(not(target_os = \"macos\"))]\n527\t    const AF_INET6: i32 = 10;\n528\t\n529\t    if addr_family == AF_INET {\n530\t        store_text_addr(\"127.0.0.1\", port)\n531\t    } else if addr_family == AF_INET6 {\n532\t        store_text_addr(\"::1\", port)\n533\t    } else {\n534\t        Err(Error::InvalidArgument)\n535\t    }\n536\t}\n537\t\n538\t/// Fill a preferred-address transport parameter from textual IPv4\n539\t/// and/or IPv6 addresses.  C: `int set_preferred_address(\n540\t/// TpPreferredAddress* preferred, char const* v4_text,\n541\t/// char const* v6_text, uint16_t preferred_port)` returning 0 / -1.\n542\t///\n543\t/// Either text argument may be `None` (the C `NULL` selector for\n544\t/// \"skip this family\").  The function mutates `preferred` in\n545\t/// place; on `Err(())` the partial state is unspecified, matching\n546\t/// the C behaviour.\n547\tpub fn set_preferred_address(\n548\t    preferred: &mut PreferredAddress,\n549\t    v4_text: Option<&str>,\n550\t    v6_text: Option<&str>,\n551\t    preferred_port: u16,\n552\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n553\t    *preferred = PreferredAddress::default();\n554\t    if let Some(v4) = v4_text {\n555\t        let addr = store_text_addr(v4, preferred_port)?;\n556\t        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V4(_)) {\n557\t            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n558\t        }\n559\t        preferred.v4 = Some(addr);\n560\t    }\n561\t    if let Some(v6) = v6_text {\n562\t        let addr = store_text_addr(v6, preferred_port)?;\n563\t        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V6(_)) {\n564\t            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n565\t        }\n566\t        preferred.v6 = Some(addr);\n567\t    }\n568\t    Ok(())\n569\t}\n570\t\n571\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n572\t// Solution-dir helpers (test fixtures).\n573\t\n574\t/// Set the global solution-relative root used by the test\n575\t/// fixtures.  C: `void set_solution_dir(char const*\n576\t/// solution_dir)`.  Passing `None` clears the override.\n577\tpub fn set_solution_dir(solution_dir: Option<&str>) {\n578\t    let mut lock = SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap();\n579\t    *lock = solution_dir.map(|s| -> &'static str { Box::leak(s.to_owned().into_boxed_str()) });\n580\t}\n581\t\n582\t/// Read the global solution-relative root.  C exposes a raw\n583\t/// `extern char const* solution_dir;` — translated as a\n584\t/// getter so the global stays behind a safe interface.\n585\tpub fn solution_dir() -> Option<&'static str> {\n586\t    *SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap()\n587\t}\n588\t\n589\t/// Compose `solution_path/file_name` (or `./file_name` when\n590\t/// `solution_path` is `None`) into `target_file_path`.  C:\n591\t/// `int get_input_path(char* target_file_path, size_t\n592\t/// file_path_max, const char* solution_path, const char*\n593\t/// file_name)`.  Buffer overflow returns `Err(())`.\n594\tpub fn get_input_path(\n595\t    target_file_path: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n596\t    solution_path: Option<&str>,\n597\t    file_name: &str,\n598\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n599\t    let solution_path = solution_path.unwrap_or(DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR);\n600\t    let sep = if solution_path.ends_with(FILE_SEPARATOR) {\n601\t        \"\"\n602\t    } else {\n603\t        FILE_SEPARATOR\n604\t    };\n605\t    write!(target_file_path, \"{}{}{}\", solution_path, sep, file_name).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)\n606\t}\n607\t\n608\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n609\t// Portable file open / close.\n610\t//\n611\t// `picoquic_file_open*` / `picoquic_file_close` only existed to\n612\t// paper over Windows's `fopen_s` quirk; the Rust translation uses\n613\t// `std::fs::File` directly at every call site (close = Drop).\n614\t//\n615\t// `picoquic_file_delete` survives because callers want a stable\n616\t// no_std-friendly façade for \"remove this file by name\".  Phase 4\n617\t// routes the body through `std::fs::remove_file` under the `std`\n618\t// feature.\n619\t\n620\t/// Delete a file by name, returning the OS errno on failure.  C:\n621\t/// `int picoquic_file_delete(char const* file_name, int* last_err)`.\n622\tpub fn file_delete(file_name: &(impl AsRef<std::path::Path> + ?Sized)) -> Result<(), i32> {\n623\t    std::fs::remove_file(file_name).map_err(|e| e.raw_os_error().unwrap_or(-1))\n624\t}\n625\t\n626\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n627\t// Frame skip / decode / encode helpers.\n628\t//\n629\t// The C entry points all share a \"cursor pair\" idiom: take\n630\t// `(bytes, bytes_max)`, advance the cursor and return a pointer\n631\t// past the parsed field, or `NULL` on overrun.  In Rust the\n632\t// `&[u8]` slice carries both endpoints; the return value is the\n633\t// remaining suffix on success or `None` on overrun (the natural\n634\t// shape of the Rust slice idiom).  Decoded values fold into the\n635\t// `Ok` payload; encoded outputs fill a pre-allocated buffer and\n636\t// return the unused tail (or `None` if the buffer was too small).\n637\t\n638\t/// Skip `size` bytes from `bytes`; returns the suffix or `None`\n639\t/// if `bytes` is too short.  C: `frames_fixed_skip`.\n640\tpub fn frames_fixed_skip(bytes: &[u8], size: u64) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n641\t    let size = usize::try_from(size).ok()?;\n642\t    bytes.get(size..)\n643\t}\n644\t\n645\t/// Skip a varint-encoded field; returns the suffix.  C:\n646\t/// `frames_varint_skip`.\n647\tpub fn frames_varint_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n648\t    let v_len = varint_len(*bytes.first()?);\n649\t    frames_fixed_skip(bytes, v_len as u64)\n650\t}\n651\t\n652\t/// Decode a varint and return the suffix plus the value.  C:\n653\t/// `frames_varint_decode` with the `*n64` out-parameter\n654\t/// folded into the tuple return.\n655\tpub fn frames_varint_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n656\t    let first = *bytes.first()?;\n657\t    let length = varint_len(first);\n658\t    if bytes.len() < length {\n659\t        return None;\n660\t    }\n661\t    let mut v = (first & 0x3F) as u64;\n662\t    for &b in &bytes[1..length] {\n663\t        v <<= 8;\n664\t        v += b as u64;\n665\t    }\n666\t    Some((&bytes[length..], v))\n667\t}\n668\t\n669\t/// Decode a length-prefixed field's length component as a\n670\t/// `usize`.  C: `frames_varlen_decode`.\n671\tpub fn frames_varlen_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], usize)> {\n672\t    let (rest, len) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n673\t    let n = usize::try_from(len).ok()?;\n674\t    Some((rest, n))\n675\t}\n676\t\n677\t/// Decode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_decode`.\n678\tpub fn frames_uint8_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u8)> {\n679\t    let (&n, rest) = bytes.split_first()?;\n680\t    Some((rest, n))\n681\t}\n682\t\n683\t/// Decode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_decode`.\n684\tpub fn frames_uint16_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u16)> {\n685\t    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n686\t        return None;\n687\t    }\n688\t    let n = u16::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1]]);\n689\t    Some((&bytes[2..], n))\n690\t}\n691\t\n692\t/// Decode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_decode`.\n693\tpub fn frames_uint32_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u32)> {\n694\t    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n695\t        return None;\n696\t    }\n697\t    let n = u32::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3]]);\n698\t    Some((&bytes[4..], n))\n699\t}\n700\t\n701\t/// Decode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_decode`.\n702\tpub fn frames_uint64_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n703\t    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n704\t        return None;\n705\t    }\n706\t    let n = u64::from_be_bytes(bytes[..8].try_into().unwrap());\n707\t    Some((&bytes[8..], n))\n708\t}\n709\t\n710\t/// Skip a length-prefixed data field.  C:\n711\t/// `frames_length_data_skip`.\n712\tpub fn frames_length_data_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n713\t    let (rest, length) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n714\t    frames_fixed_skip(rest, length)\n715\t}\n716\t\n717\t/// Decode a connection id and return the suffix.  C:\n718\t/// `frames_cid_decode`.  The output parameter folds into\n719\t/// the tuple return.\n720\tpub fn frames_cid_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], ConnectionId)> {\n721\t    let (rest, id_len) = frames_uint8_decode(bytes)?;\n722\t    if id_len as usize > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE || rest.len() < id_len as usize {\n723\t        return None;\n724\t    }\n725\t    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n726\t    id[..id_len as usize].copy_from_slice(&rest[..id_len as usize]);\n727\t    Some((&rest[id_len as usize..], ConnectionId { id, id_len }))\n728\t}\n729\t\n730\t/// Length of the varint encoding of `n64`.  C:\n731\t/// `size_t frames_varint_encode_length(uint64_t n64)`.\n732\tpub fn frames_varint_encode_length(n64: u64) -> usize {\n733\t    if n64 < 64 {\n734\t        1\n735\t    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n736\t        2\n737\t    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n738\t        4\n739\t    } else {\n740\t        8\n741\t    }\n742\t}\n743\t\n744\t/// Decode the wire length of a varint from its first byte.  C\n745\t/// macro: `VARINT_LEN(bytes) (((uint8_t)1) << ((bytes[0] >> 6) & 3))`.\n746\t/// The macro takes the whole buffer; the function takes just the\n747\t/// first byte (the rest is unused in the C expansion).\n748\tpub const fn varint_len(byte0: u8) -> usize {\n749\t    1usize << ((byte0 >> 6) & 3)\n750\t}\n751\t\n752\t/// Encode a varint into `bytes`; returns the unused tail or `None`\n753\t/// if the buffer was too small.  C: `frames_varint_encode`.\n754\tpub fn frames_varint_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n755\t    if n64 < 64 {\n756\t        if bytes.is_empty() {\n757\t            return None;\n758\t        }\n759\t        bytes[0] = n64 as u8;\n760\t        Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n761\t    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n762\t        if bytes.len() < 2 {\n763\t            return None;\n764\t        }\n765\t        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 8) | 0x40) as u8;\n766\t        bytes[1] = n64 as u8;\n767\t        Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n768\t    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n769\t        if bytes.len() < 4 {\n770\t            return None;\n771\t        }\n772\t        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 24) | 0x80) as u8;\n773\t        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n774\t        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n775\t        bytes[3] = n64 as u8;\n776\t        Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n777\t    } else {\n778\t        if bytes.len() < 8 {\n779\t            return None;\n780\t        }\n781\t        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 56) | 0xC0) as u8;\n782\t        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n783\t        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n784\t        bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n785\t        bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n786\t        bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n787\t        bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n788\t        bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n789\t        Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n790\t    }\n791\t}\n792\t\n793\t/// Encode a `usize` as a varint length prefix.  C:\n794\t/// `frames_varlen_encode`.\n795\tpub fn frames_varlen_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: usize) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n796\t    frames_varint_encode(bytes, n as u64)\n797\t}\n798\t\n799\t/// Encode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_encode`.\n800\tpub fn frames_uint8_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u8) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n801\t    if bytes.is_empty() {\n802\t        return None;\n803\t    }\n804\t    bytes[0] = n;\n805\t    Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n806\t}\n807\t\n808\t/// Encode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_encode`.\n809\tpub fn frames_uint16_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u16) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n810\t    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n811\t        return None;\n812\t    }\n813\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n814\t    bytes[1] = n as u8;\n815\t    Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n816\t}\n817\t\n818\t/// Encode a 24-bit value (low three bytes of `n`) in network\n819\t/// order.  C: `frames_uint24_encode`.\n820\tpub fn frames_uint24_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n821\t    if bytes.len() < 3 {\n822\t        return None;\n823\t    }\n824\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n825\t    bytes[1] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n826\t    bytes[2] = n as u8;\n827\t    Some(&mut bytes[3..])\n828\t}\n829\t\n830\t/// Encode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_encode`.\n831\tpub fn frames_uint32_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n832\t    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n833\t        return None;\n834\t    }\n835\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n836\t    bytes[1] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n837\t    bytes[2] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n838\t    bytes[3] = n as u8;\n839\t    Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n840\t}\n841\t\n842\t/// Encode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_encode`.\n843\tpub fn frames_uint64_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n844\t    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n845\t        return None;\n846\t    }\n847\t    bytes[0] = (n >> 56) as u8;\n848\t    bytes[1] = (n >> 48) as u8;\n849\t    bytes[2] = (n >> 40) as u8;\n850\t    bytes[3] = (n >> 32) as u8;\n851\t    bytes[4] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n852\t    bytes[5] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n853\t    bytes[6] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n854\t    bytes[7] = n as u8;\n855\t    Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n856\t}\n857\t\n858\t/// Encode a length prefix followed by the data bytes.  C:\n859\t/// `frames_length_data_encode`.  The C signature took\n860\t/// `(size_t l, const uint8_t* v)` — collapsed to `&[u8]` (slice\n861\t/// length subsumes `l`).\n862\tpub fn frames_length_data_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], v: &[u8]) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n863\t    let l = v.len();\n864\t    let rest = frames_varlen_encode(bytes, l)?;\n865\t    if rest.len() < l {\n866\t        return None;\n867\t    }\n868\t    rest[..l].copy_from_slice(v);\n869\t    Some(&mut rest[l..])\n870\t}\n871\t\n872\t/// Encode a connection id as length-prefixed data.  C:\n873\t/// `frames_cid_encode`.\n874\tpub fn frames_cid_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], cid: &ConnectionId) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n875\t    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, cid.as_bytes())\n876\t}\n877\t\n878\t/// Encode a NUL-terminated C string `s` as length-prefixed data\n879\t/// (the NUL is *not* written).  C: `frames_charz_encode`.\n880\tpub fn frames_charz_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], s: &str) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n881\t    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, s.as_bytes())\n882\t}\n883\t\n884\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n885\t// Fixed-width big-endian frame-field writers (intformat.c).\n886\t//\n887\t// Write exactly N bytes of a big-endian integer into the start of\n888\t// `bytes`, panicking if the slice is too short (same as the C\n889\t// contract: passing an undersized buffer is undefined behaviour).\n890\t\n891\t/// Write `n16` as a big-endian 16-bit value into `bytes[0..2]`.\n892\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:27-31`.\n893\tpub fn picoformat_16(bytes: &mut [u8], n16: u16) {\n894\t    bytes[0] = (n16 >> 8) as u8;\n895\t    bytes[1] = n16 as u8;\n896\t}\n897\t\n898\t/// Write the low 24 bits of `n24` as a big-endian 3-byte value into `bytes[0..3]`.\n899\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:33-38`.\n900\tpub fn picoformat_24(bytes: &mut [u8], n24: u32) {\n901\t    bytes[0] = (n24 >> 16) as u8;\n902\t    bytes[1] = (n24 >> 8) as u8;\n903\t    bytes[2] = n24 as u8;\n904\t}\n905\t\n906\t/// Write `n32` as a big-endian 32-bit value into `bytes[0..4]`.\n907\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:40-46`.\n908\tpub fn picoformat_32(bytes: &mut [u8], n32: u32) {\n909\t    bytes[0] = (n32 >> 24) as u8;\n910\t    bytes[1] = (n32 >> 16) as u8;\n911\t    bytes[2] = (n32 >> 8) as u8;\n912\t    bytes[3] = n32 as u8;\n913\t}\n914\t\n915\t/// Write `n64` as a big-endian 64-bit value into `bytes[0..8]`.\n916\t/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:48-58`.\n917\tpub fn picoformat_64(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) {\n918\t    bytes[0] = (n64 >> 56) as u8;\n919\t    bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n920\t    bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n921\t    bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n922\t    bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n923\t    bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n924\t    bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n925\t    bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n926\t}\n927\t\n928\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n929\t// Constant-time memcmp (used for reset secrets).\n930\t\n931\t/// Constant-time three-way compare of two byte buffers of equal\n932\t/// length.  C: `int constant_time_memcmp(const uint8_t*\n933\t/// x, const uint8_t* y, size_t l)` — returning negative / zero /\n934\t/// positive, mapped to [`Ordering`].  The two slice lengths are\n935\t/// the C `l` argument and must match.\n936\tpub fn constant_time_memcmp(x: &[u8], y: &[u8]) -> Ordering {\n937\t    let mut acc: u64 = 0;\n938\t    for (&xi, &yi) in x.iter().zip(y.iter()) {\n939\t        acc += (xi ^ yi) as u64;\n940\t    }\n941\t    if acc == 0 {\n942\t        Ordering::Equal\n943\t    } else {\n944\t        Ordering::Less\n945\t    }\n946\t}\n947\t\n948\t// The C `picoquic_thread_t` / `picoquic_mutex_t` / `picoquic_event_t`\n949\t// portability wrappers around pthread / Win32 are gone.  Rust call\n950\t// sites use the standard library directly: `std::thread::JoinHandle`\n951\t// for a thread handle, `std::sync::Mutex<T>` for a lock paired with\n952\t// the value it guards, and `(Mutex<bool>, Condvar)` (or a higher-level\n953\t// channel from `std::sync::mpsc`) where C used `picoquic_event_t`.\n954\t//\n955\t// Likewise the `picoquic_thread_fn` typedef has no Rust counterpart —\n956\t// `std::thread::spawn` already takes an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send`\n957\t// closure, which subsumes the C \"function pointer + void* arg\"\n958\t// pattern.\n959\t\n960\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n961\t// Random-number helpers.\n962\t\n963\t// `uniform_random` is gone -- the C body read `/dev/urandom`\n964\t// at every call.  Rust callers use `rand::Rng::random_range`\n965\t// on whatever RNG they hold (`Quic.rng` for the secure stream,\n966\t// `rand::rng()` for the non-secure thread-local).\n967\t\n968\t// `test_random` and the `test_*_random` family moved to\n969\t// `crate::tests::util` — they only support the test suite.\n970\t\n971\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n972\t// Byte-array → text conversion (logging helper).\n973\t\n974\t/// Translate a byte buffer to a printable text form.  C:\n975\t/// `char* uint8_to_str(char* text, size_t text_len, const\n976\t/// uint8_t* data, size_t data_len)` — both length parameters fold\n977\t/// into the slice lengths.  The returned slice is the populated\n978\t/// prefix of `text`.\n979\tpub fn uint8_to_str<'a>(text: &'a mut [u8], data: &[u8]) -> &'a [u8] {\n980\t    let text_len = text.len();\n981\t    let data_len = data.len();\n982\t    let render_length = if data_len >= text_len {\n983\t        text_len.saturating_sub(4)\n984\t    } else {\n985\t        data_len\n986\t    };\n987\t    let mut rendered = 0usize;\n988\t    for &c in &data[..render_length] {\n989\t        text[rendered] = if (b' '..127).contains(&c) { c } else { b'?' };\n990\t        rendered += 1;\n991\t    }\n992\t    if rendered < data_len {\n993\t        let mut i = 0usize;\n994\t        while i < 3 && rendered + 1 < text_len {\n995\t            text[rendered] = b'.';\n996\t            rendered += 1;\n997\t            i += 1;\n998\t        }\n999\t    }\n1000\t    if rendered < text_len {\n1001\t        text[rendered] = 0;\n1002\t    }\n1003\t    &text[..rendered]\n1004\t}\n1005\t\n1006\t// `TestSimPacket`, `TestAqm`, `JitterMode`, and `TestSimLink`\n1007\t// moved to `crate::tests::util` — they translate the\n1008\t// `picoquictest/sim_link.c` test-only network simulator.\n1009\t// `TEST_SNI` and the `TEST_FILE_*` / `TEST_ECH_*` cert-path\n1010\t// constants likewise moved over.\n1011\t\n1012\t#[cfg(test)]\n1013\tmod test {}\n1014\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"139b0eca-3bce-4556-91f0-6f2577288c79","uuid":"d83f7b40-3be7-4010-865e-36be74c23b78","timestamp":"2026-05-07T10:08:35.599Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/utils.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/utils.h`.\n//!\n//! Grab-bag of helpers used throughout the C library: debug\n//! tracing, sprintf-style formatting, connection-id and address\n//! manipulation, file open/close wrappers, varint and fixed-width\n//! frame field codecs, constant-time memcmp, threading primitives,\n//! a deterministic test RNG, and the test-suite's network simulator\n//! (sim_link).  No matching `.c` file lives next to the header —\n//! the bodies are in `quic/util.c` (most of the API), with a\n//! handful of helpers in `quic/utils.c`-style\n//! companions.\n//!\n//! Phase 4: all function bodies are implemented.  The empty test\n//! module will be expanded in later phases.\n//!\n//! Translation policy notes for this module:\n//!\n//! * `FILE *` → `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` for *write* sinks\n//!   (mirrors the convention introduced in\n//!   [`crate::logger`] and\n//!   [`crate::config`]).  The persistent\n//!   debug-output stream installed by [`debug_set_stream`]\n//!   transfers ownership instead, so the global slot can keep the\n//!   writer alive between calls.\n//! * `file_open*` / `file_close` collapse to direct `std::fs::File`\n//!   use at call sites; [`file_delete`] keeps the portable delete\n//!   helper as an OS-backed function.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields use\n//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`] — same convention as\n//!   [`crate`].  `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n//!   output parameters fold into `Option<SocketAddr>` (the C\n//!   `AF_UNSPEC` sentinel becomes `None`).\n//! * `int`-valued comparators (`compare_addr`,\n//!   `compare_ip_addr`, `compare_connection_id`,\n//!   `constant_time_memcmp`) return [`core::cmp::Ordering`]\n//!   when they are real three-way comparators; the `Boolean-ish`\n//!   ones (`is_connection_id_null`) become `bool`.\n//! * `char*` strings: input strings borrow as `&str`; owned outputs\n//!   return `String`.  Buffer-supplying helpers (e.g.\n//!   [`addr_text`]) take a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write`.\n//! * Threading wrappers from the C portability layer map directly to\n//!   Rust standard-library types at call sites: `std::thread`,\n//!   `std::sync::Mutex`, channels, and condition variables.\n//! * The C macro `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` and the\n//!   `DBG_PRINTF` family are bodies-not-signatures: they expand at\n//!   the call site rather than being declared in the header.  They\n//!   are call-site macros rather than standalone functions; translated\n//!   call sites record equivalent wake provenance when their owning\n//!   connection or QUIC context exposes it.\n\nuse core::cmp::Ordering;\nuse core::net::{IpAddr, SocketAddr};\nuse std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::sync::Mutex;\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::{CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE, ConnectionId, PreferredAddress};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Tracing / file-id constants.\n//\n// Used by the C `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` macro to record\n// which translation unit last poked the QUIC context.  The macro\n// itself is translated at the C macro's call sites.\n\n/// File-id constant for `sender.c`.\npub const SENDER: u32 = 1;\n/// File-id constant for `packet.c`.\npub const PACKET: u32 = 2;\n/// File-id constant for `quicctx.c`.\npub const QUICCTX: u32 = 3;\n/// File-id constant for `frames.c`.\npub const FRAME: u32 = 4;\n/// File-id constant for `loss_recovery.c`.\npub const LOSS_RECOVERY: u32 = 5;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Rate / time / byte conversion (function-like macros in C).\n\n/// Bytes that fit on a link of `bps` bytes per second over\n/// `microseconds`.  C: `BYTES_FROM_RATE`.\npub const fn bytes_from_rate(microseconds: u64, bps: u64) -> u64 {\n    microseconds.wrapping_mul(bps) / 1_000_000\n}\n\n/// Effective rate (bytes per second) implied by sending `bytes`\n/// in `microseconds`.  C: `RATE_FROM_BYTES`.\npub const fn rate_from_bytes(bytes: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {\n    bytes.wrapping_mul(1_000_000) / microseconds\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Filesystem path constants — Linux/macOS layout (the only target\n// supported in v1; `_WINDOWS` paths are dropped per\n// `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).\n\n/// Default solution-relative path used by the test fixtures.\npub const DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR: &str = \"./\";\n/// Path separator used to assemble fixture paths.\npub const FILE_SEPARATOR: &str = \"/\";\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Debug-printf knobs and tracing helpers.\n\n/// Maximum number of trailing characters of `__FILE__` shown by\n/// the C `DBG_PRINTF` macro (`__FILE__` is right-truncated to the\n/// last `DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX` bytes).\npub const DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX: usize = 24;\n\n// Thread-local debug state — no Send required; library is single-threaded.\nthread_local! {\n    static DEBUG_OUT: RefCell<Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>> = const { RefCell::new(None) };\n    static DEBUG_SUSPENDED: Cell<bool> = const { Cell::new(false) };\n}\n\n// Global solution_dir; typically set once at startup.  Box::leak gives the\n// returned reference a 'static lifetime at the cost of a one-time allocation.\nstatic SOLUTION_DIR: Mutex<Option<&'static str>> = Mutex::new(None);\n\n/// Install (or clear, with `None`) the global debug-output sink.\n/// The C signature `void debug_set_stream(FILE *F)` takes a\n/// borrowed `FILE*`; Rust keeps the sink alive across calls by\n/// transferring ownership of a `Box<dyn Write>`.  Phase 3 will\n/// decide whether to expose a borrowed-with-`'static`-lifetime\n/// alternative.\npub fn debug_set_stream(stream: Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>) {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| *o.borrow_mut() = stream);\n}\n\n/// `printf`-style write to the installed debug sink.  The C\n/// variadic signature `void debug_printf(const char* fmt, ...)`\n/// becomes a sink for already-formatted text — Rust call sites\n/// will use `format_args!` / `write!` directly.  The string is\n/// borrowed for the duration of the call.\npub fn debug_printf(msg: &str) {\n    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n        return;\n    }\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n            let _ = w.write_str(msg);\n        }\n    });\n}\n\n/// Push the current debug sink and install a new one.  Mirrors\n/// `void debug_printf_push_stream(FILE* f)`.  The previously\n/// installed sink (if any) is saved for [`debug_printf_pop_stream`]\n/// to restore.\npub fn debug_printf_push_stream(stream: Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>) {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n        assert!(\n            guard.is_none(),\n            \"nested debug_printf_push_stream not supported\"\n        );\n        *guard = Some(stream);\n    });\n}\n\n/// Pop the most recently pushed debug sink and restore the saved\n/// one.  Mirrors `void debug_printf_pop_stream(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_pop_stream() {\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        let mut guard = o.borrow_mut();\n        assert!(\n            guard.is_some(),\n            \"debug_printf_pop_stream: no current stream\"\n        );\n        *guard = None;\n    });\n}\n\n/// Stop logging through the debug sink without tearing it down.\n/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_suspend(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_suspend() {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(true));\n}\n\n/// Resume logging through the previously installed debug sink.\n/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_resume(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_resume() {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.set(false));\n}\n\n/// Force the debug suspension flag to `suspended` and return the\n/// previous value.  C: `int debug_printf_reset(int suspended)`.\n/// The flag is a 0/1 indicator on both sides, so it maps to\n/// `bool`.\npub fn debug_printf_reset(suspended: bool) -> bool {\n    DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| {\n        let old = s.get();\n        s.set(suspended);\n        old\n    })\n}\n\n/// Hex-dump `bytes` to the debug sink.  C: `void debug_dump(const\n/// void *x, int len)` — only declared when `_DEBUG` is defined,\n/// so this entry point is informational in v1.  The C `int len`\n/// is non-negative in every observed call; mapped to slice length.\npub fn debug_dump(bytes: &[u8]) {\n    if DEBUG_SUSPENDED.with(|s| s.get()) {\n        return;\n    }\n    DEBUG_OUT.with(|o| {\n        if let Some(w) = o.borrow_mut().as_mut() {\n            for (i, chunk) in bytes.chunks(16).enumerate() {\n                let _ = write!(w, \"{:04x}:  \", i * 16);\n                for b in chunk {\n                    let _ = write!(w, \"{:02x} \", b);\n                }\n                let _ = writeln!(w);\n            }\n        }\n    });\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// String utilities.\n\n// `picoquic_string_create`, `picoquic_string_duplicate`, and\n// `picoquic_string_free` are subsumed by Rust's owned `String`:\n// `String::from(s)` replaces the duplicate / create pair, and the\n// `Drop` impl replaces the `free` shim.  Phase 3 callers should use\n// owned `String` directly rather than going through helpers.\n\n/// Format `args` into the head of `buf` and report bytes written\n/// via `nb_chars`; truncation returns an `Err`.  C:\n/// `int sprintf(char* buf, size_t buf_len, size_t* nb_chars,\n/// const char* fmt, ...)`.\n///\n/// The C variadic signature collapses to a single\n/// pre-formatted `&str` argument, mirroring the convention used\n/// by [`debug_printf`].  Buffer-overflow returns `Err(())`\n/// (matching the C `-1`); on success the `Ok(usize)` is the byte\n/// count written, replacing the `nb_chars` out-parameter.\npub fn sprintf(buf: &mut [u8], msg: &str) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n    let bytes = msg.as_bytes();\n    if bytes.len() < buf.len() {\n        buf[..bytes.len()].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n        buf[bytes.len()] = 0;\n        Ok(bytes.len())\n    } else if !buf.is_empty() {\n        let n = buf.len() - 1;\n        buf[..n].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..n]);\n        buf[n] = 0;\n        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n    } else {\n        Err(Error::BufferTooSmall)\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Connection-id helpers.\n\n/// All-zero connection id used as a sentinel for \"unset\" / \"none\".\n/// C: `extern const ConnectionId null_connection_id`.\npub const NULL_CONNECTION_ID: ConnectionId = ConnectionId {\n    id: [0; 20],\n    id_len: 0,\n};\n\n/// Format the connection id into `bytes` and return the number of\n/// bytes written.  C: `uint8_t format_connection_id(uint8_t* bytes,\n/// size_t bytes_max, ConnectionId connection_id)`.\n///\n/// Pointer-shape choice: the C body writes through `bytes` for\n/// `bytes_max` bytes and reports the populated prefix length, so\n/// `&mut [u8]` carries both pieces of information.  The connection\n/// id is `Copy`, kept by value as in C.\npub fn format_connection_id(bytes: &mut [u8], cnx_id: ConnectionId) -> u8 {\n    let id_bytes = cnx_id.as_bytes();\n    let copied = id_bytes.len();\n    if copied == 0 || copied > bytes.len() {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    bytes[..copied].copy_from_slice(id_bytes);\n    copied as u8\n}\n\n/// Parse a connection id of `len` bytes from `bytes` into `connection_id`,\n/// returning the number of bytes consumed.  C:\n/// `uint8_t parse_connection_id(const uint8_t* bytes, uint8_t len,\n/// ConnectionId* connection_id)`.\n///\n/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n/// `Result<ConnectionId, Error>` reports the parsed id on\n/// success, `Err(())` on truncation.  The `len` parameter and the\n/// slice length are redundant in safe Rust; the slice carries it.\npub fn parse_connection_id(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n    let len = bytes.len();\n    if len > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n        return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n    }\n    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n    id[..len].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n    Ok(ConnectionId {\n        id,\n        id_len: len as u8,\n    })\n}\n\n// `is_null` / cmp / `hash_with_seed` / `val64` live as methods on\n// [`ConnectionId`] (see `crate::lib`); they were free fns in the\n// C source.  `Ord` for `ConnectionId` derives the lexicographic\n// byte-then-length order the C `compare_connection_id` produced.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Address helpers.\n\n/// Pack an IP-and-port into a stable, byte-form key suitable for\n/// hashing.  C: `size_t hash_addr_bytes(const struct\n/// sockaddr* addr, uint8_t* bytes)`.  Returns the populated prefix\n/// length; the C body writes at most 18 bytes (16 for the v6\n/// address + 2 for the port).\npub fn hash_addr_bytes(addr: &SocketAddr, bytes: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n    let mut l = 0;\n    match addr {\n        SocketAddr::V4(a) => {\n            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n            bytes[l..l + 4].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n            l += 4;\n            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n            l += 2;\n        }\n        SocketAddr::V6(a) => {\n            let ip = a.ip().octets();\n            bytes[l..l + 16].copy_from_slice(&ip);\n            l += 16;\n            let port = addr.port().to_ne_bytes();\n            bytes[l..l + 2].copy_from_slice(&port);\n            l += 2;\n        }\n    }\n    l\n}\n\n/// Hash an address with a 16-byte seed.  C: `uint64_t\n/// hash_addr(const struct sockaddr* addr, const uint8_t*\n/// hash_seed)`.\npub fn hash_addr(addr: &SocketAddr, hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n    let mut bytes = [0u8; 18];\n    let l = hash_addr_bytes(addr, &mut bytes);\n    crate::siphash::siphash(&bytes[..l], hash_seed)\n}\n\n/// Decode a hex-coded byte string and write the binary form into\n/// `bin_output`.  C: `size_t parse_hexa(char const*\n/// hex_input, size_t input_length, uint8_t* bin_output, size_t\n/// output_max)`.  Returns the number of bytes decoded.\n///\n/// Both length parameters fold into the slice lengths.\npub fn parse_hexa(hex_input: &str, bin_output: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n    fn hexa_digit(x: u8) -> Option<u8> {\n        match x {\n            b'0'..=b'9' => Some(x - b'0'),\n            b'A'..=b'F' => Some(x - b'A' + 10),\n            b'a'..=b'f' => Some(x - b'a' + 10),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n    let inp = hex_input.as_bytes();\n    let input_length = inp.len();\n    if input_length == 0 || (input_length & 1) != 0 || 2 * bin_output.len() < input_length {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    let mut ret = 0;\n    let mut offset = 0;\n    while offset < input_length {\n        match (hexa_digit(inp[offset]), hexa_digit(inp[offset + 1])) {\n            (Some(av), Some(bv)) => {\n                bin_output[ret] = (av << 4) | bv;\n                ret += 1;\n            }\n            _ => return 0,\n        }\n        offset += 2;\n    }\n    ret\n}\n\n/// Parse a hex-coded connection id.  C: `uint8_t\n/// parse_connection_id_hexa(char const* hex_input, size_t\n/// input_length, ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning the\n/// number of bytes decoded; the output parameter folds into the\n/// `Result` return.\npub fn parse_connection_id_hexa(hex_input: &str) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n    let len = parse_hexa(hex_input, &mut id[..18]);\n    Ok(ConnectionId {\n        id,\n        id_len: len as u8,\n    })\n}\n\n/// Print a connection id to a hex string buffer.  C:\n/// `int print_connection_id_hexa(char* buf, size_t buf_len,\n/// const ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning 0 / -1.\n///\n/// The output buffer becomes a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` sink to\n/// keep the helper `no_std`-friendly (same convention as the\n/// logger module).  The 0 / -1 status maps to `Result<(), Error>`.\npub fn print_connection_id_hexa(\n    w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n    connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    for b in connection_id.as_bytes() {\n        write!(w, \"{:02x}\", b).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)?;\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare two addresses (IP + port).  C:\n/// `int compare_addr(const struct sockaddr* expected,\n/// const struct sockaddr* actual)`.\npub fn compare_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n    let ip_cmp = compare_ip_addr(expected, actual);\n    if ip_cmp == Ordering::Equal {\n        expected.port().cmp(&actual.port())\n    } else {\n        ip_cmp\n    }\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare just the IP component of two addresses.  C:\n/// `int compare_ip_addr(const struct sockaddr*, const\n/// struct sockaddr*)`.\npub fn compare_ip_addr(expected: &SocketAddr, actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n    match (expected, actual) {\n        (SocketAddr::V4(ex), SocketAddr::V4(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n        (SocketAddr::V6(ex), SocketAddr::V6(ac)) => ex.ip().octets().cmp(&ac.ip().octets()),\n        // AF_INET (2) < AF_INET6 (10 on Linux), so V4 < V6.\n        (SocketAddr::V4(_), SocketAddr::V6(_)) => Ordering::Less,\n        (SocketAddr::V6(_), SocketAddr::V4(_)) => Ordering::Greater,\n    }\n}\n\n/// Read the port number out of an address.  C: `uint16_t\n/// get_addr_port(const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\npub fn get_addr_port(addr: &SocketAddr) -> u16 {\n    addr.port()\n}\n\n/// Replace the port number on an address.  C declares this with\n/// `const struct sockaddr*` but the body casts away const and\n/// writes through the pointer (`util.c:531`).  The Rust signature\n/// takes a `&mut SocketAddr` to reflect the actual contract.\npub fn set_addr_port(addr: &mut SocketAddr, port: u16) {\n    addr.set_port(port);\n}\n\n/// Length of the platform sockaddr representation in bytes —\n/// `sizeof(sockaddr_in)` for v4, `sizeof(sockaddr_in6)` for v6,\n/// `0` for `AF_UNSPEC`.  C: `int addr_length(const struct\n/// sockaddr* addr)`.  The return value is non-negative; mapped to\n/// `usize`.\npub fn addr_length(addr: &SocketAddr) -> usize {\n    match addr {\n        SocketAddr::V4(_) => 16, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)\n        SocketAddr::V6(_) => 28, // sizeof(struct sockaddr_in6)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Copy an address into a sockaddr_storage slot, treating\n/// `addr == None` as the C \"zero out the storage\" path.  C:\n/// `void store_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n/// const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n///\n/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n/// `Option<SocketAddr>` represents stored / cleared respectively.\npub fn store_addr(addr: Option<&SocketAddr>) -> Option<SocketAddr> {\n    addr.copied()\n}\n\n/// Return the IP-bytes (4 or 16) inside a sockaddr.  C:\n/// `void get_ip_addr(struct sockaddr* addr, uint8_t**\n/// ip_addr, uint8_t* ip_addr_len)` — both output parameters fold\n/// into the returned slice.  `None` matches the C path where\n/// `*ip_addr = NULL; *ip_addr_len = 0;` for unsupported families.\npub fn get_ip_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    // `SocketAddr` exposes IP octets only by value (`IpAddr::octets()`),\n    // not by reference; safe Rust cannot produce the required borrow.\n    // Callers should use `addr.ip()` with `Ipv4Addr::octets()` /\n    // `Ipv6Addr::octets()` directly.\n    None\n}\n\n/// Parse `ip_address_text` (IPv4 or IPv6 textual form) and combine\n/// with `port` into a [`SocketAddr`].  C:\n/// `int store_text_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n/// const char* ip_address_text, uint16_t port)` returning 0 / -1.\npub fn store_text_addr(ip_address_text: &str, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n    let ip: IpAddr = ip_address_text\n        .parse()\n        .map_err(|_| Error::InvalidArgument)?;\n    Ok(SocketAddr::new(ip, port))\n}\n\n/// Print an address (IP + port) to a `core::fmt::Write` sink.  C:\n/// `char const* addr_text(const struct sockaddr* addr,\n/// char* text, size_t text_size)` returns the populated prefix of\n/// `text`; the Rust translation writes through a sink to stay\n/// `no_std`-friendly.  The `Err` arm propagates a fmt error.\npub fn addr_text(addr: &SocketAddr, w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write) -> Result<(), core::fmt::Error> {\n    match addr {\n        SocketAddr::V4(a) => write!(w, \"{}:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n        SocketAddr::V6(a) => write!(w, \"[{}]:{}\", a.ip(), a.port()),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Build the loopback address for the given family + port.  C:\n/// `int store_loopback_addr(struct sockaddr_storage*\n/// stored_addr, int addr_family, uint16_t port)` — the family\n/// argument is one of `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6` and folds into the\n/// `SocketAddr` variant.\n///\n/// `addr_family` stays an `i32` to keep parity with the C ABI;\n/// callers in the codebase pass the `AF_*` constants directly.\npub fn store_loopback_addr(addr_family: i32, port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n    // AF_INET=2 is universal; AF_INET6=10 on Linux, 30 on macOS.\n    const AF_INET: i32 = 2;\n    #[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]\n    const AF_INET6: i32 = 30;\n    #[cfg(not(target_os = \"macos\"))]\n    const AF_INET6: i32 = 10;\n\n    if addr_family == AF_INET {\n        store_text_addr(\"127.0.0.1\", port)\n    } else if addr_family == AF_INET6 {\n        store_text_addr(\"::1\", port)\n    } else {\n        Err(Error::InvalidArgument)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Fill a preferred-address transport parameter from textual IPv4\n/// and/or IPv6 addresses.  C: `int set_preferred_address(\n/// TpPreferredAddress* preferred, char const* v4_text,\n/// char const* v6_text, uint16_t preferred_port)` returning 0 / -1.\n///\n/// Either text argument may be `None` (the C `NULL` selector for\n/// \"skip this family\").  The function mutates `preferred` in\n/// place; on `Err(())` the partial state is unspecified, matching\n/// the C behaviour.\npub fn set_preferred_address(\n    preferred: &mut PreferredAddress,\n    v4_text: Option<&str>,\n    v6_text: Option<&str>,\n    preferred_port: u16,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    *preferred = PreferredAddress::default();\n    if let Some(v4) = v4_text {\n        let addr = store_text_addr(v4, preferred_port)?;\n        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V4(_)) {\n            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n        }\n        preferred.v4 = Some(addr);\n    }\n    if let Some(v6) = v6_text {\n        let addr = store_text_addr(v6, preferred_port)?;\n        if !matches!(addr, SocketAddr::V6(_)) {\n            return Err(Error::InvalidArgument);\n        }\n        preferred.v6 = Some(addr);\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Solution-dir helpers (test fixtures).\n\n/// Set the global solution-relative root used by the test\n/// fixtures.  C: `void set_solution_dir(char const*\n/// solution_dir)`.  Passing `None` clears the override.\npub fn set_solution_dir(solution_dir: Option<&str>) {\n    let mut lock = SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap();\n    *lock = solution_dir.map(|s| -> &'static str { Box::leak(s.to_owned().into_boxed_str()) });\n}\n\n/// Read the global solution-relative root.  C exposes a raw\n/// `extern char const* solution_dir;` — translated as a\n/// getter so the global stays behind a safe interface.\npub fn solution_dir() -> Option<&'static str> {\n    *SOLUTION_DIR.lock().unwrap()\n}\n\n/// Compose `solution_path/file_name` (or `./file_name` when\n/// `solution_path` is `None`) into `target_file_path`.  C:\n/// `int get_input_path(char* target_file_path, size_t\n/// file_path_max, const char* solution_path, const char*\n/// file_name)`.  Buffer overflow returns `Err(())`.\npub fn get_input_path(\n    target_file_path: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n    solution_path: Option<&str>,\n    file_name: &str,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    let solution_path = solution_path.unwrap_or(DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR);\n    let sep = if solution_path.ends_with(FILE_SEPARATOR) {\n        \"\"\n    } else {\n        FILE_SEPARATOR\n    };\n    write!(target_file_path, \"{}{}{}\", solution_path, sep, file_name).map_err(|_| Error::Generic)\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Portable file open / close.\n//\n// `picoquic_file_open*` / `picoquic_file_close` only existed to\n// paper over Windows's `fopen_s` quirk; the Rust translation uses\n// `std::fs::File` directly at every call site (close = Drop).\n//\n// `picoquic_file_delete` survives because callers want a stable\n// no_std-friendly façade for \"remove this file by name\".  Phase 4\n// routes the body through `std::fs::remove_file` under the `std`\n// feature.\n\n/// Delete a file by name, returning the OS errno on failure.  C:\n/// `int picoquic_file_delete(char const* file_name, int* last_err)`.\npub fn file_delete(file_name: &(impl AsRef<std::path::Path> + ?Sized)) -> Result<(), i32> {\n    std::fs::remove_file(file_name).map_err(|e| e.raw_os_error().unwrap_or(-1))\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Frame skip / decode / encode helpers.\n//\n// The C entry points all share a \"cursor pair\" idiom: take\n// `(bytes, bytes_max)`, advance the cursor and return a pointer\n// past the parsed field, or `NULL` on overrun.  In Rust the\n// `&[u8]` slice carries both endpoints; the return value is the\n// remaining suffix on success or `None` on overrun (the natural\n// shape of the Rust slice idiom).  Decoded values fold into the\n// `Ok` payload; encoded outputs fill a pre-allocated buffer and\n// return the unused tail (or `None` if the buffer was too small).\n\n/// Skip `size` bytes from `bytes`; returns the suffix or `None`\n/// if `bytes` is too short.  C: `frames_fixed_skip`.\npub fn frames_fixed_skip(bytes: &[u8], size: u64) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    let size = usize::try_from(size).ok()?;\n    bytes.get(size..)\n}\n\n/// Skip a varint-encoded field; returns the suffix.  C:\n/// `frames_varint_skip`.\npub fn frames_varint_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    let v_len = varint_len(*bytes.first()?);\n    frames_fixed_skip(bytes, v_len as u64)\n}\n\n/// Decode a varint and return the suffix plus the value.  C:\n/// `frames_varint_decode` with the `*n64` out-parameter\n/// folded into the tuple return.\npub fn frames_varint_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n    let first = *bytes.first()?;\n    let length = varint_len(first);\n    if bytes.len() < length {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut v = (first & 0x3F) as u64;\n    for &b in &bytes[1..length] {\n        v <<= 8;\n        v += b as u64;\n    }\n    Some((&bytes[length..], v))\n}\n\n/// Decode a length-prefixed field's length component as a\n/// `usize`.  C: `frames_varlen_decode`.\npub fn frames_varlen_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], usize)> {\n    let (rest, len) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n    let n = usize::try_from(len).ok()?;\n    Some((rest, n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint8_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u8)> {\n    let (&n, rest) = bytes.split_first()?;\n    Some((rest, n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint16_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u16)> {\n    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let n = u16::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1]]);\n    Some((&bytes[2..], n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint32_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u32)> {\n    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let n = u32::from_be_bytes([bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3]]);\n    Some((&bytes[4..], n))\n}\n\n/// Decode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_decode`.\npub fn frames_uint64_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let n = u64::from_be_bytes(bytes[..8].try_into().unwrap());\n    Some((&bytes[8..], n))\n}\n\n/// Skip a length-prefixed data field.  C:\n/// `frames_length_data_skip`.\npub fn frames_length_data_skip(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    let (rest, length) = frames_varint_decode(bytes)?;\n    frames_fixed_skip(rest, length)\n}\n\n/// Decode a connection id and return the suffix.  C:\n/// `frames_cid_decode`.  The output parameter folds into\n/// the tuple return.\npub fn frames_cid_decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], ConnectionId)> {\n    let (rest, id_len) = frames_uint8_decode(bytes)?;\n    if id_len as usize > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE || rest.len() < id_len as usize {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n    id[..id_len as usize].copy_from_slice(&rest[..id_len as usize]);\n    Some((&rest[id_len as usize..], ConnectionId { id, id_len }))\n}\n\n/// Length of the varint encoding of `n64`.  C:\n/// `size_t frames_varint_encode_length(uint64_t n64)`.\npub fn frames_varint_encode_length(n64: u64) -> usize {\n    if n64 < 64 {\n        1\n    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n        2\n    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n        4\n    } else {\n        8\n    }\n}\n\n/// Decode the wire length of a varint from its first byte.  C\n/// macro: `VARINT_LEN(bytes) (((uint8_t)1) << ((bytes[0] >> 6) & 3))`.\n/// The macro takes the whole buffer; the function takes just the\n/// first byte (the rest is unused in the C expansion).\npub const fn varint_len(byte0: u8) -> usize {\n    1usize << ((byte0 >> 6) & 3)\n}\n\n/// Encode a varint into `bytes`; returns the unused tail or `None`\n/// if the buffer was too small.  C: `frames_varint_encode`.\npub fn frames_varint_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if n64 < 64 {\n        if bytes.is_empty() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n    } else if n64 < 16384 {\n        if bytes.len() < 2 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 8) | 0x40) as u8;\n        bytes[1] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n    } else if n64 < 1073741824 {\n        if bytes.len() < 4 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 24) | 0x80) as u8;\n        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n        bytes[3] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n    } else {\n        if bytes.len() < 8 {\n            return None;\n        }\n        bytes[0] = ((n64 >> 56) | 0xC0) as u8;\n        bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n        bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n        bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n        bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n        bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n        bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n        bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n        Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n    }\n}\n\n/// Encode a `usize` as a varint length prefix.  C:\n/// `frames_varlen_encode`.\npub fn frames_varlen_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: usize) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    frames_varint_encode(bytes, n as u64)\n}\n\n/// Encode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint8_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u8) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = n;\n    Some(&mut bytes[1..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint16_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u16) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 2 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[2..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a 24-bit value (low three bytes of `n`) in network\n/// order.  C: `frames_uint24_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint24_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 3 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[3..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint32_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 4 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[4..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_encode`.\npub fn frames_uint64_encode(bytes: &mut [u8], n: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n    if bytes.len() < 8 {\n        return None;\n    }\n    bytes[0] = (n >> 56) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n >> 48) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n >> 40) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = (n >> 32) as u8;\n    bytes[4] = (n >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[5] = (n >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[6] = (n >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[7] = n as u8;\n    Some(&mut bytes[8..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a length prefix followed by the data bytes.  C:\n/// `frames_length_data_encode`.  The C signature took\n/// `(size_t l, const uint8_t* v)` — collapsed to `&[u8]` (slice\n/// length subsumes `l`).\npub fn frames_length_data_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], v: &[u8]) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n    let l = v.len();\n    let rest = frames_varlen_encode(bytes, l)?;\n    if rest.len() < l {\n        return None;\n    }\n    rest[..l].copy_from_slice(v);\n    Some(&mut rest[l..])\n}\n\n/// Encode a connection id as length-prefixed data.  C:\n/// `frames_cid_encode`.\npub fn frames_cid_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], cid: &ConnectionId) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, cid.as_bytes())\n}\n\n/// Encode a NUL-terminated C string `s` as length-prefixed data\n/// (the NUL is *not* written).  C: `frames_charz_encode`.\npub fn frames_charz_encode<'a>(bytes: &'a mut [u8], s: &str) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n    frames_length_data_encode(bytes, s.as_bytes())\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Fixed-width big-endian frame-field writers (intformat.c).\n//\n// Write exactly N bytes of a big-endian integer into the start of\n// `bytes`, panicking if the slice is too short (same as the C\n// contract: passing an undersized buffer is undefined behaviour).\n\n/// Write `n16` as a big-endian 16-bit value into `bytes[0..2]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:27-31`.\npub fn picoformat_16(bytes: &mut [u8], n16: u16) {\n    bytes[0] = (n16 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = n16 as u8;\n}\n\n/// Write the low 24 bits of `n24` as a big-endian 3-byte value into `bytes[0..3]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:33-38`.\npub fn picoformat_24(bytes: &mut [u8], n24: u32) {\n    bytes[0] = (n24 >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n24 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = n24 as u8;\n}\n\n/// Write `n32` as a big-endian 32-bit value into `bytes[0..4]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:40-46`.\npub fn picoformat_32(bytes: &mut [u8], n32: u32) {\n    bytes[0] = (n32 >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n32 >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n32 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = n32 as u8;\n}\n\n/// Write `n64` as a big-endian 64-bit value into `bytes[0..8]`.\n/// C: `picoquic/intformat.c:48-58`.\npub fn picoformat_64(bytes: &mut [u8], n64: u64) {\n    bytes[0] = (n64 >> 56) as u8;\n    bytes[1] = (n64 >> 48) as u8;\n    bytes[2] = (n64 >> 40) as u8;\n    bytes[3] = (n64 >> 32) as u8;\n    bytes[4] = (n64 >> 24) as u8;\n    bytes[5] = (n64 >> 16) as u8;\n    bytes[6] = (n64 >> 8) as u8;\n    bytes[7] = n64 as u8;\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Constant-time memcmp (used for reset secrets).\n\n/// Constant-time three-way compare of two byte buffers of equal\n/// length.  C: `int constant_time_memcmp(const uint8_t*\n/// x, const uint8_t* y, size_t l)` — returning negative / zero /\n/// positive, mapped to [`Ordering`].  The two slice lengths are\n/// the C `l` argument and must match.\npub fn constant_time_memcmp(x: &[u8], y: &[u8]) -> Ordering {\n    let mut acc: u64 = 0;\n    for (&xi, &yi) in x.iter().zip(y.iter()) {\n        acc += (xi ^ yi) as u64;\n    }\n    if acc == 0 {\n        Ordering::Equal\n    } else {\n        Ordering::Less\n    }\n}\n\n// The C `picoquic_thread_t` / `picoquic_mutex_t` / `picoquic_event_t`\n// portability wrappers around pthread / Win32 are gone.  Rust call\n// sites use the standard library directly: `std::thread::JoinHandle`\n// for a thread handle, `std::sync::Mutex<T>` for a lock paired with\n// the value it guards, and `(Mutex<bool>, Condvar)` (or a higher-level\n// channel from `std::sync::mpsc`) where C used `picoquic_event_t`.\n//\n// Likewise the `picoquic_thread_fn` typedef has no Rust counterpart —\n// `std::thread::spawn` already takes an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send`\n// closure, which subsumes the C \"function pointer + void* arg\"\n// pattern.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Random-number helpers.\n\n// `uniform_random` is gone -- the C body read `/dev/urandom`\n// at every call.  Rust callers use `rand::Rng::random_range`\n// on whatever RNG they hold (`Quic.rng` for the secure stream,\n// `rand::rng()` for the non-secure thread-local).\n\n// `test_random` and the `test_*_random` family moved to\n// `crate::tests::util` — they only support the test suite.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Byte-array → text conversion (logging helper).\n\n/// Translate a byte buffer to a printable text form.  C:\n/// `char* uint8_to_str(char* text, size_t text_len, const\n/// uint8_t* data, size_t data_len)` — both length parameters fold\n/// into the slice lengths.  The returned slice is the populated\n/// prefix of `text`.\npub fn uint8_to_str<'a>(text: &'a mut [u8], data: &[u8]) -> &'a [u8] {\n    let text_len = text.len();\n    let data_len = data.len();\n    let render_length = if data_len >= text_len {\n        text_len.saturating_sub(4)\n    } else {\n        data_len\n    };\n    let mut rendered = 0usize;\n    for &c in &data[..render_length] {\n        text[rendered] = if (b' '..127).contains(&c) { c } else { b'?' };\n        rendered += 1;\n    }\n    if rendered < data_len {\n        let mut i = 0usize;\n        while i < 3 && rendered + 1 < text_len {\n            text[rendered] = b'.';\n            rendered += 1;\n            i += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    if rendered < text_len {\n        text[rendered] = 0;\n    }\n    &text[..rendered]\n}\n\n// `TestSimPacket`, `TestAqm`, `JitterMode`, and `TestSimLink`\n// moved to `crate::tests::util` — they translate the\n// `picoquictest/sim_link.c` test-only network simulator.\n// `TEST_SNI` and the `TEST_FILE_*` / `TEST_ECH_*` cert-path\n// constants likewise moved over.\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod test {}\n","numLines":1014,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1014}}}
